{"id":3758,"date":"2021-09-24T09:31:13","date_gmt":"2021-09-24T16:31:13","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=3758"},"modified":"2023-08-21T08:35:44","modified_gmt":"2023-08-21T15:35:44","slug":"3-6-timers","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/3-6-timers\/","title":{"rendered":"3.6: Timers"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214a6c136b\" name=\"6a2214a6c136b\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Goals and Steps to Succes<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Goals:<\/p>\n<p>By the end of this lesson, I will be able to:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Explain how computers measure time<\/li>\n<li>Describe what an epoch time is and how it impacts time calculations<\/li>\n<li>Describe the calculations that occur when we add or subtract time<\/li>\n<li>Store and use time values in variables and lists in a program<\/li>\n<li>Use variables to store times and report the duration of flight movements and programs<\/li>\n<li>Limit program time using timers<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Steps to Success:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>How Does the Timer Work?<\/li>\n<li>Total Flight Timer<\/li>\n<li>Movement Timer<\/li>\n<li>Using Timers in a Function<\/li>\n<li>Challenge 1: One Minute Code<\/li>\n<li>Challenge 2: LED Flight Time Warning<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214a6c156a\" name=\"6a2214a6c156a\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>How Does the Timer Work?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Timers are everywhere so they might not seem like a big deal, but being able to use a timer in your program is an incredibly useful and powerful skill! This can help you keep better track of your flights and the movements within them.<\/span><\/p>\n<p>You may remember back when we first learned how to import and create our drone that we discussed\u00a0<strong>objects.\u00a0<\/strong>We also mentioned that objects have\u00a0<strong>properties\u00a0<\/strong>and\u00a0<strong>methods.\u00a0<\/strong>Much like our drone object we create in each program is a virtual representation of our physical drone, we can create a timer object that keeps track of time much like a timer on your phone or watch would.<\/p>\n<p>We have already used the\u00a0<em><strong>time\u00a0<\/strong><\/em>library before when we made our drone <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">time.sleep(seconds)<\/code>. Now we will use it to check and the time at various points in our code and perform some simple math. Before we do this, let&#8217;s take a look at the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">time.time()<\/code> function and see how it works and what value it gets:<\/p>\n<div style=\"width: 1200px;\" class=\"wp-video\"><video class=\"wp-video-shortcode\" id=\"video-3758-1\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" preload=\"metadata\" controls=\"controls\"><source type=\"video\/mp4\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/09\/timer-practice.mp4?_=1\" \/><a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/09\/timer-practice.mp4\">https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/09\/timer-practice.mp4<\/a><\/video><\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214a6c1611\" name=\"6a2214a6c1611\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Total Flight Timer<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">For this activity, you will be programming a timer to keep track of your CoDrone Mini\u2019s total flight, starting at takeoff and finishing with landing.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">After importing the CoDrone Mini and time libraries, creating the drone object, and pairing, create a variable to store the drone\u2019s starting time (we called ours \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">start_time<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">). Have your drone take off and land, and then create another variable to store the drone\u2019s ending time (we called ours \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">end_time<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">).<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">To get the total time of your drone\u2019s flight, create a variable that will subtract <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">start_time<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> from <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">end_time<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">. We called ours \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">elapsed_time<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">. Finally, use a print command to have your program print the elapsed time in seconds.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Once you have tried this out, add hovering and then some flight movements into your program. What changes do you notice?<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your code should look something like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\nimport time\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\nstart_time = time.time()\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\n\r\ndrone.land()\r\n\r\nend_time = time.time()\r\n\r\n\r\nelapsed_time = end_time - start_time\r\n\r\nprint(elapsed_time)\r\n\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214a6c16b2\" name=\"6a2214a6c16b2\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Movement Timer<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">What if you wanted to check how long one section in your program took, like how long your drone takes to fly in one square, or if pitching forward for 2 seconds actually takes 2 seconds? There\u2019s a way to do that, and it just involves moving some timers around in your program!<\/span><\/p>\n<p>For this exercise, we will pretend that we are programming a movie videography drone. The director wants a 360 degree continuous shot around the two actors for an epic final scene! They need at least 30 seconds of video to cut into the movie. For this program, we will create a loop that lasts 30 seconds using a time and fly in a circle around the actors.<\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">After importing the CoDrone Mini, Sequence and time libraries, creating the drone object, and pairing, have your drone take off.<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Next, we need create the variables for the loop. We will create a start_time that will capture the time right before the loop. We will also create an end_time and assign it the start_time variable value + 30 seconds for the required flight time:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">import CoDrone_mini\r\nfrom CoDrone_mini import Sequence\r\nimport time\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\ndrone.takeoff\r\n\r\nstart_time = time.time()\r\nend_time= start_time + 30\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>Now, we need to create the loop. To do this, we will compare the start_time to the end_time and as long as it is less than the end time, we keep running the circle. Note that we are using the fly_sequence function. You can program your own circular flight path if you choose, but if not, make sure you have the correct imports as shown above.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">while start_time &lt; end_time:\r\n    drone.fly_sequence(Sequence.CIRCLE)\r\n    start_time = time.time() #update the time once the sequence is done\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>We have to update the start_time after each sequence or it would end up creating an infinite loop. Can you figure out why?<\/p>\n<p>Now, have your drone land. To check if our flight time was enough, add a line that prints out the difference between start time and end time. Did we fly long enough? Was it exactly 30 seconds? If the time wasn&#8217;t exact, why do you think this is the case?<\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214a6c1730\" name=\"6a2214a6c1730\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Using Timers in a Function<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Creating multiple variables to time one thing can be a lot. In cases when you want to time one movement, you can create a function instead! This will run a timer for the \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">move()<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">function for however many seconds you put in the parameters.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">After importing the CoDrone Mini and time libraries, creating the drone object, and pairing, define a function (we called ours \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">fly()<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">) and put <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">seconds<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> in the parameters. Next, create a variable to store the drone\u2019s starting time. Create a while loop so that if the time minus the start time is less than the seconds in the parameters, the drone will move.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Next, have your drone take off and set the pitch to 30. Include the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">fly()<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u00a0function and set the parameters to 2 seconds, and then have the drone land.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Play around with the time in the parameters. What happens? Does increasing or decreasing the pitch have an impact on the time?<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your code should look something like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\nimport time\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\n# -------- function definition ---------\r\n\r\ndef fly(seconds):\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 start_time = time.time()\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 while time.time() - start_time &lt; seconds:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.move()\r\n\r\n# --------------------------------------\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\n\r\ndrone.set_pitch(30)\r\n\r\nfly(2)\r\n\r\ndrone.land()\r\n\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214a6c17a7\" name=\"6a2214a6c17a7\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge 1: One Minute Code<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Fit as much flight movement as you can into one minute exactly (according to the \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">elapsed_time<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">variable in your program)!<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Rules:<\/span><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You must include at least one pitch, one throttle, one yaw, and one roll.<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Any flight movement, including hovering or delaying, can only last for 3 seconds.<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The same movement cannot be used in consecutive order, whether that is one after the other or in a loop (so don\u2019t loop pitching forward for 3 seconds 20 times).<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Takeoff and landing are included in the one minute.<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214a6c180a\" name=\"6a2214a6c180a\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge 2: LED Flight Time Warning<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]As we have discussed in previous lessons, our drone uses its LED to communicate important information. For this challenge, create a flight sequence with at least 5 movements. Use random variable values to plug in for the move function and duration for each flight movement. Once the program is complete, have the drone land and then flash an LED light sequence that tells you how long (approximately) the flight time was in 15 second increments. For example:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Blue &lt; 15 seconds<\/li>\n<li>Red between 15 and 30 seconds<\/li>\n<li>Green between 30 and 45 seconds<\/li>\n<li>Orange between 45 seconds and minute<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 8 Goals and Steps to Succes [vc_column_text]Goals: By the end of this lesson, I will be able to: Explain how computers measure time Describe what an epoch time&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/3-6-timers\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":4146,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":3473,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1782,1848,1793,1826,1482,853,694,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2135,2125,2122,2134,2139],"class_list":["post-3758","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-beginner","lesson-tag-codrone-mini","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","lesson-tag-timers","module-level-3","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/3758","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":9,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/3758\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":9107,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/3758\/revisions\/9107"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/4146"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=3758"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=3758"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=3758"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}